			},
			solution: {
				'index.html': `<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>{{title}}<\/title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
  <\/head>
  <body>
    <header class="site-header" id="top">
      <p class="brand">{{name}}<\/p>
      <nav aria-label="Site sections">
        <ul>
          <li><a href="#sec-1">{{sections.0}}<\/a><\/li>
          <li><a href="#sec-2">{{sections.1}}<\/a><\/li>
          <li><a href="#sec-3">{{sections.2}}<\/a><\/li>
        <\/ul>
      <\/nav>
    <\/header>

    <main id="main">
      <h1>{{name}}<\/h1>
      <p>{{who}}<\/p>
      <p><a class="button" href="#enquiry">{{mainAction.label}}<\/a><\/p>

      <section id="sec-1">
        <h2>{{sections.0}}<\/h2>
        <p>A couple of sentences about this section.<\/p>
      <\/section>

      <section id="sec-2">
        <h2>{{sections.1}}<\/h2>
        <p>A couple of sentences about this section.<\/p>
      <\/section>

      <section id="sec-3">
        <h2>{{sections.2}}<\/h2>
        <p>A couple of sentences about this section.<\/p>
      <\/section>

      <section id="offer">
        <h2>What we offer<\/h2>
        <div class="cards">
          {{#each items}}<article class="card">
            <h3>{{title}}<\/h3>
            <p>{{text}}<\/p>
            <p class="price">{{price}}<\/p>
          <\/article>
          {{/each}}
        <\/div>
      <\/section>

      <section id="enquiry">
        <h2>{{mainAction.label}}<\/h2>
        <form action="/enquiry" method="post">
          <p>
            <label for="f-name">What should we call you?<\/label><br>
            <input id="f-name" name="name" type="text" autocomplete="name" required>
          <\/p>
          <p>
            <label for="f-phone">Phone<\/label><br>
            <input id="f-phone" name="phone" type="tel" autocomplete="tel" required>
          <\/p>
          <p><button type="submit">{{mainAction.label}}<\/button><\/p>
        <\/form>
      <\/section>
    <\/main>

    <section data-scaffold id="choose">
      <h2>Which store for which feature<\/h2>
      <p>Kinds of store: relational database, document, key-value, graph or a plain file. If one relational database is enough, say so.<\/p>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Project feature<\/th><th>Kind of store<\/th><th>Why<\/th><th>What we pay<\/th><\/tr>
        <tr class="fn catalog"><td>Catalog of services or products (items)<\/td><td class="store">relational database (PostgreSQL)<\/td><td class="why">there are few services, they share the same fields, and selecting and sorting are needed<\/td><td class="cost">nothing: this is the most ordinary case for tables<\/td><\/tr>
        <tr class="fn requests"><td>Visitors’ enquiries: {{mainAction.label}}<\/td><td class="store">relational database (PostgreSQL)<\/td><td class="why">an enquiry refers to a service, reports and transactions are needed, and this is personal data too<\/td><td class="cost">nothing: links and transactions are exactly what is needed here<\/td><\/tr>
        <tr class="fn sessions"><td>Sessions of signed-in visitors<\/td><td class="store">key-value (Redis)<\/td><td class="why">a session has to be found instantly by the token from a cookie, and it has a lifetime<\/td><td class="cost">one more server to maintain; after a crash the sessions are lost and the person has to sign in again<\/td><\/tr>
        <tr class="fn links"><td>Links between the project’s people (people)<\/td><td class="store">relational database (PostgreSQL)<\/td><td class="why">there are few people and few links, so a link table and a JOIN are enough<\/td><td class="cost">if the chains of links become many, queries get complex; then a graph is worth a look<\/td><\/tr>
        <tr class="fn counter"><td>A fast visitor counter for today<\/td><td class="store">key-value (Redis)<\/td><td class="why">the counter grows with every visit, and an atomic increment in memory is very fast<\/td><td class="cost">the data is in memory: after a crash the counter may reset, so it is not for money<\/td><\/tr>
      <\/table>
      <p>Where the data lives and who can read it: <span id="where">everything sits on one server in an EU data center: PostgreSQL and Redis behind a closed port; the owner can read it through the admin panel, and so can the site’s server, and the database ports are not open to the outside<\/span><\/p>
      <p>Where I chose a relational database although I was tempted by another, and why: <span id="boring">for the catalog and the enquiries: I was tempted by a document database, but enquiries are tied to services, and reports and transactions are needed, which a relational database does<\/span><\/p>
    <\/section>


    <footer class="site-footer">
      <p>{{name}}, {{city}}<\/p>
    <\/footer>
  <\/body>
<\/html>
`,
				'styles.css': `/* styles.css: the page after the CSS part, abbreviated */

