`
			},
			checks: [
				{ id: 'choose-exists', kind: 'exists', selector: '#choose' },
				{ id: 'no-blanks', kind: 'text', selector: '#choose', matches: '^[^…]*$' },
				{ id: 'functions', kind: 'count', selector: '#choose tr.fn', min: 5 },
				{
					id: 'store-named',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#choose td.store',
					matches: '[Rr]elational|SQL|Postgres|SQLite|MySQL|[Dd]ocument|Mongo|[Kk]ey|Redis|[Gg]raph|Neo4j|[Ff]ile'
				},
				{ id: 'relational-used', kind: 'text', selector: '#choose td.store', matches: '[Rr]elational|SQL|Postgres|SQLite|MySQL' },
				{ id: 'reasons', kind: 'text', selector: '#choose td.why', matches: '\\S+\\s+\\S+\\s+\\S+\\s+\\S+' },
				{
					id: 'cost-named',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#choose td.cost',
					matches: '[Ll]ink|[Rr]elation|[Tt]ransaction|JOIN|[Ss]erver|[Mm]emory|[Nn]othing|[Ll]imit|[Tt]ool|[Rr]eport|[Mm]aintain|[Mm]aintenance|[Cc]omplex|[Qq]uer|[Cc]rash|[Ll]ost|[Dd]uplicat'
				},
				{ id: 'where-readers', kind: 'text', selector: '#where', matches: '[Ss]erver|[Cc]loud|[Hh]ost|[Cc]ountry|[Rr]ead|[Aa]ccess|[Aa]dmin|[Pp]assword|EU|[Rr]egion' },
				{ id: 'boring-choice', kind: 'text', selector: '#boring', matches: '(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:[Rr]elational|PostgreSQL|SQL))(?=[\\s\\S]*\\S+\\s+\\S+\\s+\\S+\\s+\\S+)' }
			]
		},
		quiz: [
			{
				type: 'match',
				prompt: 'Match each kind of database to the job it suits best',
				pairs: [
					['Relational (PostgreSQL)', 'catalogs, enquiries, orders: linked data with reports and transactions'],
					['Document (MongoDB)', 'records with different fields, read whole in one piece'],
					['Key-value (Redis)', 'sessions, caches and counters: fast by key, often with a lifetime'],
					['Graph (Neo4j)', 'links several steps deep: friends of friends, recommendations, fraud detection']
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'Where is it best to keep the sessions of signed-in visitors: a token from a cookie, and a lifetime of half an hour?',
				options: [
					'In a key-value store: a record is found instantly by its token, and the lifetime can be set',
					'In a graph database: a session is a link between a person and the site',
					'In a separate document inside the enquiries table'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. The key is the token, the value is the session. Redis does it in a fraction of a millisecond and deletes the expired ones itself. But a small site can perfectly well keep sessions in an ordinary table too.',
					'No: graphs are about links several steps deep, and a session has no links.',
					'No: sessions shouldn’t be mixed with enquiries, because they have different lifetimes and different secrecy.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'A small site: a catalog of thirty services, enquiries with contact details, a monthly report. What is the default choice?',
				options: [
					'One relational database, for example PostgreSQL or SQLite',
					'MongoDB for the catalog and Redis for the enquiries',
					'A graph database, because services are linked to people'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. Linked data, reports and transactions are what relational databases were invented for. And JSON columns cover the rare “pieces with any fields”.',
					'No: you get two databases instead of one and lose the links between enquiries and services.',
					'No: there are few people and few services, and links one step deep are handled perfectly well by a JOIN.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'multiple',
				prompt: 'What do you pay for choosing a document database for data that is linked together?',
				options: [
					'You have to check links and integrity yourself: there is no JOIN or foreign key of the familiar kind',
					'Data is often duplicated across several documents, and has to be fixed everywhere',
					'Reports and complex selections have to be assembled by hand',
					'The database stops storing JSON'
				],
				answer: [0, 1, 2],
				why: [
					'Yes: the main price of flexibility is that nobody tidies up for you.',
					'Yes: by nesting a service in every enquiry you get the very update anomalies from lesson 3.',
					'Yes: reports are a strong side of SQL, and a weaker one in document databases.',
					'No: on the contrary, JSON is the native format of a document database.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'You need “friends of friends of friends” for recommendations, and there are millions of such links. Which store fits?',
				options: [
					'A graph database: walking edges several steps deep is its native job',
					'Key-value: under a person’s key sits the list of their friends',
					'A JSON file'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. When links are the main thing and the steps are many, a graph is faster and simpler: the query reads like a path along edges. But most sites have no such case.',
					'No: getting one person’s friends is easy, but a walk three steps down the chain has to be done by hand, calling the store many times.',
					'No: the file would have to be read whole for every question.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'Why not give every feature of the site its own “best-suited” database?',
				options: [
					'Every new database is one more server to run, update, secure and back up, and one more point of failure',
					'Databases of different kinds can’t be installed on the same server',
					'The law forbids using more than one database'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. All that work falls on you, and on a small site the “best-suited” database most often brings no gain. The rule: take another database once you have measured that you can’t manage without it.',
					'No: they can, and it is often done.',
					'No: there is no such law.'
				]
			}
		],
		cards: [
			{
				id: 'data-nosql-meaning',
				front: 'What does NoSQL mean, and when are such databases appropriate?',
				back: '“Not only SQL”: databases that store data other than in tables, such as documents, key-value pairs and graphs. They fit when a task has a special shape: pieces with different fields, fast counters and sessions, links several steps deep.'
			},
			{
				id: 'data-nosql-document',
				front: 'A document database: what does it store, and what does it lose?',
